==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kelh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|claritee}}, from {{der|en|fro|clarté}}, from {{der|en|la|clāritās}}, from {{m|la|clārus|t=clear}}; equivalent to {{suf|en|clear|ity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈklæɹ.ɪ.ti/|/ˈklæɹ.ə.ti/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-clarity.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈklæɹ.ɪ.ti/||/ˈklɛɹ.ə.ti/}}
* {{a|AusE}} {{IPA|en|/ˈklæɹ.ə.ti/}}
* {{rhymes|en|æɹɪti|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state or measure of being [[clear]], either in [[appearance]], [[thought]] or [[style]]; [[lucidity]].
#: {{ux|en|She dreamed with great '''clarity'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The brilliant '''clarity''' of his argument could not be faulted.}}
# The ability to be easily understood.
#: {{ux|en|Lack of '''clarity''' on the part of the teacher will cause confusion among the students.}}
#: {{syn|en|clearness|obviousness|transparency}}
#: {{ant|en|confusion}}
# The ability to think clearly and rationally.
# The ability to be easily heard.
#: ''The singer was praised for the amazing '''clarity''' in her voice.''

